ohn has been one of the most important  figures in history but also  one of the most meaningful people that  i've ever met  in my life and these moments where we  spent  at the very end of his life or were so  important for me i wanted to express my  appreciation and my love for this  this great man but also to tell him how  much he had meant to me and  its death was imminent we both knew that  um but we somehow found peace in  in his transition in knowing what he had  meant to america  and what he meant to me and my family  such an extraordinary man who  rose from such humble roots abject  poverty  in rural alabama and yet he rose to be  one of the most powerful and beloved  figures in the 20th century it's a great  story for  everyone who doubts whether they can  achieve  and wants to overcome it they always  under appreciate it  and and and i think never really gave  john his his due because he was such a  small  unassuming man but what a powerful  impact he had on our lives  that's what i feel too that he was so  that i wonder if he knew how much people  loved and cared about him i wonder if he  was able  to take all of that in do you think he  was  gayle i'm certain that he was i spent a  lot of time with john  in different places around the country  and everywhere  john lewis went without bodyguards  without staff  people flocked to him and it was so  heartwarming for me  because i knew what he had done in his  life  and and it just it was so wonderful to  see people  touch him and love him and express that  to him  and uh it was just i think the most  remarkable journey of john's life and  his life story  you know i've said often and it's not  hyperbole uh gail  that um when i met john lewis as a young  lawyer  from duke um your son's alma mater  i thought it was a transcendent  experience it was like  meeting the great figures of history uh  thomas jefferson  benjamin franklin the the people that  wrote the declaration of independence  and  here i was meeting someone who had made  america bend and and live up to those  noble words john  lewis ct vivian another lion that we  just lost or  or andy young dr king it was uh  it was a remarkable life and i was  privileged to be able to to live  alongside of him  you know what struck me too about him  you know i called to check on him last  weekend oprah and i did and  and michael said you know you all should  talk to him he'd love to talk to you on  the phone we're like no no no we can't  and we talked him on the phone last  saturday and oprah was saying you've  been such a blessing in my life  and he said to her no oprah you've been  a blessing in mind and we both just had  a moment with that because  even at the end bill even at the end  he's thinking about others  and trying to uplift others i just  thought that was so extraordinary  well think about this gail john  used his last public appearance in life  to go and stand in the black lives  matter plaza  he had been told that the cancer had  ravaged his body  he did not have long to live and yet he  was able to get  up and go and stand in that plaza  to express to the young people of  america how powerful their voices had  been  and the changes that they had made in  america which he had fought for  his entire life and i i think when you  when you look at john's life and his  life arc  and you see that it was always about  making america better  he had this incorruptible integrity like  a halo  and it just made all of us better who  knew him  and your your phone call and oprah's  phone call and  and uh speaker pelosi and president  obama and vice president biden  the winans saying to john uh  over the phone it just made his uh his  last moment  special yes and we keep hearing that he  loves  hillary clinton was saying we all know  that he was joyful  he loved the song happy by pharrell you  two must have had some playful moments  that were unique to just the two of you  do you have an antidote about that yes  of course we  first of all you know we shared an  office for uh for five years then we  gotta go  yes john lewis only learned to drive in  his seventies  and the first time he wanted to show me  that he could drive  he ran through a stop sign and i said  john what happened he said oh i didn't  even recognize  it was there so he was just such a  wonderful human being to be around  and we will miss him a great deal  we really will he made us all so happy  we thank you mayor bill campbell for  joining us really good to see you i'm  sorry it's this way

